## .NET 8 Migration: `TimedExecutor`

### Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET Core+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also removed** — the entire cooperative abort model is gone
3. The idiomatic replacement is `CancellationToken` + `Task` with timeout

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block with the same semantics as the original thread.Join(timeout)
            return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Async-first overload (preferred in modern .NET)
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Caller Migration

```csharp
// BEFORE (.NET Framework)
var executor = new TimedExecutor();
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(
    () => SlowDatabaseQuery(),
    TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));

// AFTER (.NET 8) — work must respect the token
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(
    ct => SlowDatabaseQuery(ct),
    TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Thread.Abort was "rude"** | It could interrupt *anywhere*. `CancellationToken` is cooperative — your `work` delegate **must** check `ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` or pass the token to I/O calls, or it won't actually stop. |
| **Uncooperative work** | If you truly can't modify the work (e.g., calling a 3rd-party blocking API), the thread will keep running even after timeout. There is no safe equivalent to `Abort()` in .NET 8. |
| **`default` return value** | The original code could return `default(T)` after abort — this was already a latent bug. The new version never reaches that path. |
| **Exception wrapping** | The original wrapped in `AggregateException`. `Task.Run` does this naturally; `GetAwaiter().GetResult()` unwraps it, preserving the original exception type. Match whichever behavior callers expect. |
| **Thread pool starvation** | The original used a dedicated thread. `Task.Run` uses the ThreadPool. For truly long-blocking work, consider `Task.Factory.StartNew` with `TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning`. |

### For Uncooperative Work (Last Resort)

If the work *cannot* accept a `CancellationToken` (e.g., opaque third-party call):

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeoutUncooperative<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    // Fire-and-forget on a background thread — the thread LEAKS on timeout
    var task = Task.Factory.StartNew(work, TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning);

    if (task.Wait(timeout))
        return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();

    // Thread keeps running — log this, it's a resource leak
    throw new TimeoutException(
        $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout (thread leaked)");
}
```

> ⚠️ This is strictly worse than the original — it **leaks the thread** instead of aborting it. Use only as a stopgap while migrating the underlying work to support cancellation.
